{"id": "1.2-00023", "case_id": "github_schoolmips_simple_direct_v1", "parent_id": null, "source": "github/mipsfpga/schoolmips", "record_protocol": null, "panels": {"stored": "<h2>Agent task and scoring</h2><div class='card'><table><tbody><tr><th style='width:175px'>Input task</th><td class='path'>Project specification, interfaces, and permitted public assets</td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Agent context</th><td class='path'>Direct agent workspace: <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00023&amp;file_q=public%2F'>public/</a> (9 files)</td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Expected output</th><td class='path'>Required RTL: src/sm_cpu.v, src/sm_hex_display.v, src/sm_register.v, src/sm_rom.v, src/sm_top.v. <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00023&amp;file=public%2Foutput_contract.json'>Open full output contract</a></td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Hidden reference</th><td class='path'>5 RTL files under private/reference/; <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00023&amp;file=private%2Freference%2Fsrc%2Fsm_cpu.v'>open first reference file</a></td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Private score</th><td class='path'>Passed verification testpoints divided by total testpoints, using testbenches or formal verification<br><strong>1 verification testpoints:</strong> schoolmips_cpu. <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00023&amp;file=private%2Fscoring.json'>Open private scoring contract</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Case-stored agent instruction</h2><div class='card'><p class='muted'>These are case-stored instructions. A launcher may add framing or execution limits; its saved trajectory is the exact runtime record.</p><details><summary>public/instruction.md · <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00023&amp;file=public%2Finstruction.md'>Open raw</a></summary><pre># schoolMIPS 00_simple project\nImplement the complete sm_top integration and all modules at the five required paths. Read the unchanged original schoolMIPS English slides, Russian user manual, root README and wiki Home under specification/. The project author documents the simple single-cycle architecture and supported instruction subset; later branches with data memory, interrupts, pipelining and AHB are excluded.\n\nCurator clarification of the pinned source and original verification contract:\n- The supported instruction subset is exactly addu, or, srl, sltu, subu, addiu, lui, beq and bne. Use standard MIPS encodings shown in the supplied original slides. No data memory, jump, delay slot or architectural exception is required. PC is a 32-bit WORD index; normal next PC is PC+1 and a taken branch adds the sign-extended 16-bit immediate to PC+1. ADDU/SUBU/ADDIU wrap modulo 2^32; ADDIU sign-extends its immediate, SLTU compares unsigned, SRL shifts the second register operand logically by shamt. Architectural register zero reads zero even if targeted by a write. Unsupported instructions do not write a register and advance PC.\n- sm_cpu&#x27;s combinational imAddr/imData interface fetches the current instruction. Each rising CPU clock commits one instruction. Active-low rst_n asynchronously clears the PC through sm_register. Register-file words have no reset port and are unspecified until software writes them; the original simulation fixture initializes its register-file array to zero. Debug regAddr=0 reads word PC (not architectural zero); other addresses read register values asynchronously.\n- sm_top synchronizes clkDevide, clkEnable and regAddr with two clkIn registers, drives sm_clk_divider, connects a 64-word sm_rom to sm_cpu and exports the CPU clock and debug data. The divider counts enabled clkIn edges in 32 bits and selects counter bit shift+devide (default shift=16), unless bypass=1 directly forwards clkIn. Reset clears the counter. Hold control inputs stable while observing debug outputs; the scored native bench uses bypass=1.\n- sm_rom exposes an asynchronous 32-bit word lookup indexed by a, parameter SIZE=64. Its implementation must load exactly the supplied runtime program.hex via initial $readmemh(&quot;program.hex&quot;, rom). This constant filename is the only permitted simulation input initialization. No design is provided by this file: it contains test firmware. CPU logic must execute arbitrary supported instructions, not recognize the bundled programs. Out-of-range ROM reads are unspecified. sm_register and sm_register_we are parameter-width active-low asynchronous-reset registers, the latter with write enable. sm_hex_display is the auxiliary combinational hex display module at its declared path; it is required by the source contract although the system trace bench does not independently score it.\n- Native-bench compatibility names are REQUIRED: sm_top must instantiate the CPU as sm_cpu and divider as sm_clk_divider; CPU must expose the current 32-bit instruction as instr and instantiate its register file as rf, with reg [31:0] rf [31:0] inside it. The original bench initializes and traces these names and sets sm_top.sm_clk_divider.bypass=1. These are explicitly declared fixture constraints, not hidden requirements on arbitrary implementations. No other internal naming or state encoding is constrained.\n- Header specification/support/sm_cpu.vh preserves opcode and ALU constants. It can be included using `include &quot;sm_cpu.vh&quot; and is supplied privately to compilation unchanged; it contains constants only. All five implementation files are collected from the submission and replace every golden counterpart before scoring.\n\nThe one original system testbench is one testpoint. All three original firmware configurations (counter, Fibonacci and square root) must complete their recorded 120-cycle trace, including PC, instruction and register v0. This timing-sensitive educational single-cycle contract is intentional. These programs do not establish exhaustive instruction or peripheral correctness.\n</pre></details></div>"}, "files": [{"name": "case.json", "url": "assets/6618e0c29dd942ff7607e7da885ff5cefb107595d0817a717a0a92c8f06fea4e.